    "## 4.5.3 · November 2026",
    "- **Autoplay that knows you:** the songs autoplay and song radios bring are put in an order that fits you: songs you often play after the one playing, artists you love and the ones you play alongside them, your own copies, songs that sound alike (tempo and loudness) or share the mood or genre, and the artists you play at this time of day and on this output (the car, earbuds, the PC). Left out: anything heard in the last three hours, songs you keep skipping (above all in their first seconds), and what you said no to. No artist twice in a row.",
    "- **More like this, Less like this, Not for autoplay:** in a song's Options and an artist's menu. Autoplay, radios and the DJ follow them, and they sync between your phone and computer.",
    "- **The DJ learns too:** it leaves out what you said no to and what you keep skipping straight away; its artists for you lean on the artists you play together; and artists whose new songs keep getting skipped aren't brought again.",
    "- **Stats › Taste:** your phases (new loves, rising, long-time favourites, faded), the artists you skip most, the songs you always hear to the end, the new songs the DJ and autoplay brought and how they went, and everything you said, each undone with a tap.",
    "- **Thirty effects that move to the music** (Effects › Moves to the music): beat rings, lasers, a disco ball, flames, a heart monitor, bass lightning and more, reacting to the bass, the highs and the beat, as the visualizers do.",
    "- **Music scenes:** thirty new looks in a group of their own, each a kind of music (Jazz club, Punk, Disco, Techno, Concert hall, City pop…), each with an effect that moves to the music.",
    "- **Rearrange:** a playlist's songs (Rearrange songs, in its Options) and your playlists (Rearrange…, with the Your order sort): drag them, or move one place at a time.",
    "- **Add to playlist** shows only your own playlists: no discovery playlists, artist playlists, weekly mixes, smart playlists or Listen later.",
    "- **Bit-perfect (the phone):** twice as much sound is kept queued with the USB DAC, for the stutter when pulling down the notifications or opening the recent apps; any gap left is written down in Settings › Problems with its cause. A false \"music not getting through\" note when a song started over is gone.",
    "",
    "## 4.5.2 · November 2026",
    "- **Bit-perfect: no more loud static on FLACs (the phone):** with bit-perfect on, the phone maker's FLAC decoder (Samsung's and others') gave 16-bit FLACs as loud static, whatever the sound went to, the phone's speaker too. With bit-perfect on, FLACs now always go through Android's own decoder, which gives them right.",
    "- **Bit-perfect: the app and the phone stop pulling the USB DAC between them (the phone):** taking the DAC over for bit-perfect made some phones' USB drop it and find it again, over and over: the sound switching between the DAC and the speaker every second or two, static or silence, the DAC's screen changing all the time. Each drop also cleared the count that should have stopped it, so it never stopped. Now, after a few takeovers in a minute, the app stops taking the DAC and the music plays through Android (whole, but mixed by Android); Settings says why, and turning bit-perfect off and on tries again.",
    "- **Bit-perfect: no gaps when Android slows the app for a moment (the phone):** opening the recent apps (or switching apps) made the music stop for a second with the DAC set up again. Android slowing the app for a moment was taken for the DAC stopping (after just 0.15 s), and only 0.4 s of music was kept ready. Now 1.2 s is kept ready, and the stream is only set up again after 0.8 s with nothing from the DAC.",
    "",
    "## 4.5.1 · November 2026",
    "- **Around the cover, thirty new visualizers:** in Personalization, Around the cover takes the place of 4.5.0's Middle visualizer under the cover switch. Switch it on and pick one of thirty made to go round the album cover on Now playing, from its edge outward and never over it: Glow, Bars, Fine lines, Blocks, Wave, Twin wave, Dots, Orbits, Comet, Twin comets, Ripples, Echo, Neon frame, Marching lights, Spectrum ring, Spikes, Flames, Petals, Sun rays, Corona, Sparkles, Lightning, Heartbeat, Ribbon, Beads, Side bars, Above and below, Halo, Pulse shadow and Particles. Each follows the cover's shape and size from the layout (square, rounded, very round, a circle like a record). While it's on, no middle visualizer is drawn; the one along the bottom still is. Its colour is in Colours, and its menu shows each one moving round a cover in your layout's shape.",
    "- **Dual visualizer, with its own two pickers:** with Dual on, In the middle and Along the bottom each have a picker, in place of Now playing visualizer's one.",
    "- **Your own, round the cover:** the visualizer maker has an Around the cover switch: on, you make one from the thirty that go round the cover (and only those), with your own Spin, Strength and colour; it goes in Around the cover's menu and no other. Off, you make one from the Visualizer's styles as before, in its menus. Around the cover's menu has Make your own, and yours can be changed or deleted from it.",
    "- **Dual's middle can go round the cover:** In the middle offers the ones that go round the cover (yours too) as well as the ones over it.",
    "- **Colours shows what you'd get:** each colour in Colours is shown first on the visualizer it's for, moving, on Now playing and by itself: tap once to see it, again to pick it.",
    "- **The lyrics scan only looks for the songs still without lyrics:** it went through every song, reading each file again to skip the ones with lyrics. Now the songs whose file has lyrics, the ones kept in the app, and the ones left alone or instrumental aren't gone through at all, and its count is only of the songs it looks for.",
    "- **Save edits to one of your presets:** beside Save as a preset (Personalization presets), Save edits toÃÂ¢ÃÂÃÂ¦ lists your presets, the one you put on last first: pick one and what you have on now is saved over it, its name kept, after a question. No need to open it with Edit first, which put it back as it was.",
    "- **Milestones, a tab of their own:** Statistics has Milestones: every milestone you've reached, each song and artist getting to 100, 250, 500, 1,000ÃÂ¢ÃÂÃÂ¦ plays, with the day the play that got it there happened. Songs, artists or both, newest first (by year) or biggest first. Tap a song of yours to play it, an artist to open their page. Most played still shows the last month's. Every song and artist that reached one is in, with no limit (before, only the top 3,000 songs and 1,000 artists were looked at).",
    "- **A stuck sync frees itself:** a sync that never finished (the network dropping partway, the app put away while it worked) held the phone's syncing for good, so Sync now on the PC waited for ever with nothing said. It's let go after 8 minutes and noted in Settings âº Problems, and a sync that can't start says why.",
    "- **Forget on the phone works:** with Play on already off, tapping Forget (Settings › Remote & sync) forgot the PC but the screen kept showing \"Paired with â¦\", so it seemed to do nothing and you couldn't pair again. The screen changes straight away now.",
    "- **Pairing only pairs:** pairing the phone with the PC used to switch Play on on (stopping the phone's own music) and could start a sync straight away, from a Sync now tapped on the PC before, or Auto playlist sync meeting the PC. Now pairing does nothing else: Play on stays off until you switch it on, and nothing syncs until you tap Sync now (Auto playlist sync, if it's on, only syncs changes made after pairing).",
    "",
    "## 4.5.0 · November 2026",
    "- **Other device's songs sees through soundtrack notes:** a song whose name on one device has its film in it (\"Lose Yourself (From \"8 Mile\")\", \"â¦ - From the Motion Picture â¦\") and on the other not is the same song now, so it isn't listed as missing.",
    "- **The middle visualizer over or under the cover:** a new switch beside Dual visualizer (Personalization), Middle visualizer under the cover: on, a visualizer in the middle of Now playing is drawn behind the album cover and shows round its edges; off (as before), it's drawn over the cover.",
    "- **Lyrics already in your files count:** songs whose files already hold their lyrics (copied over from your other device, or written by another app) are counted as having them, in Settings › Lyrics and the Moods tab, and the lyrics scan leaves them alone. Before, they showed as \"still to find\" until a scan went through them. Each file is looked at once, in the background.",
    "- **Colours, all in one place:** Personalization has a Colours section: every visualizer's colour at once, or each one in use on its own (Now playing's middle and bottom ones, the Visualizer tab's), and each effect you have on. A visualizer you made keeps the colour you made it with as its default; change it here any time without making a new one or editing it, and As it comes puts it back. The effects' colours moved here from under Effects.",
    "- **Effects previewed together:** in the Effects menu, the effect you look at is shown with the others you have on, each in its own colour, so you see exactly how they would all look at once.",
    "- **A shorter, clearer Features page:** the Features page (â° menu) and the README on GitHub are written again, about half as long: every feature still in, each said simply, with how to switch it off or change it.",
    "- **Lighter:** what the app no longer uses is taken out: three screens nothing opened any more (the old Single scan, the old tag scanner and Plays on singles and albums), what was left of the jump-to-a-letter strip (long lists still worked out its letters, unseen, each time they were drawn), and a dozen unused pieces. Nothing you use changes.",
    "- **The song playing is safe on Windows too:** a song's file named two ways (its path, and a file:// address) wasn't seen as the same file on Windows, so a scan could write to the song playing. It's recognised however it's written now.",
    "- **Update always gets the newest version:** the app looks for an update when it starts and every 12 hours, and Update used to install the version that look had found, even when a newer one had come out since (4.0.37 went in after 4.0.38 was out, so it asked to update twice). Update looks again as you tap it and installs the newest one out, on the phone and the PC.",
    "",
    "## 4.0.38 · November 2026",
    "- **Moods already in your files count:** a song whose file holds moods written somewhere else (on your other device, then the music copied over) isn't counted as waiting for Scan moods any more, and Scan moods leaves it alone: it doesn't hear it again, read its words (with the AI or without) or write over its moods. Before, music copied over with its moods showed every song as still to scan. Settings › Reset mood and lyrics scans still has them all scanned again.",
    "- **Never show on Similar artists, Duplicates in Most played and Joint artists:** for one that's right as it is. It changes nothing and leaves it out of that list for good, with Undo right after; \"Show them again\" at the top of the list, or Settings › Never show › Reset Never show, brings them back.",
    "- **Everything you pick in the scans goes across with Sync:** Never show in every scan, Folders to leave alone, the songs kept as live, as a soundtrack note or alone in their album, and Not a duplicate went across before but named the files as the sending device writes them (the phone and the PC write the music folder differently), so the other device never matched them. They're put under the other device's own music folder now, and used straight away: some were only used after the app was started again. Your stats fixes (merges, joint artists' picks, Keep apart, Leave alone) went across already.",
    "- **The scans' tag changes go across with Sync:** what Fix albums and artists, Live, Soundtrack notes, songs alone in an album, Fix genre names and Identify by sound change in a song's tags is made to the other device's copy too, as your own Edit tags changes are, with Tags match off as well. Moving files into their folders stays each device's own.",
    "",
    "## 4.0.37 · November 2026",
    "- **Empty folders on the phone:** with All files access (Settings › Tagger) the phone has the Empty folders scan too: folders in your music folders with no songs left in them, only pictures, lyrics files and the like, each shown with its size and what's in it, to tick and delete. A phone has no Recycle Bin, so a folder you delete there is gone for good with what's left in it: the setting and the confirmation say so, and each folder is looked at again just before and kept if any music is in it. Your top Music folder and Folders to leave alone are never touched.",
    "- **Cover searches find more:** \"EP\" as a word anywhere in an album's name and a year at its end (\"Lone Survivor EP 2013\") are left off the search too, as \" - EP\", \"Single\", brackets and slashes already were.",
    "- **Tidying after a move, on the phone:** when songs are moved or deleted, the folders they leave with nothing at all in them are removed across your music folders, not only in the app's download folder.",
    "",
    "## 4.0.36 · November 2026",
    "- **The file scans on the phone too:** Fix albums and artists, Live in song names, Soundtrack notes in names and Folders to leave alone are in the phone's Settings now, as on the PC (your own files need All files access, Settings › Tagger). The folders you leave alone are left alone on the phone too. Moving files to their right folders works on the phone too, through Android's own list of music: each song keeps its address, so your playlists, queue, favorites, plays and sync never see it change; never over a file that's there already, and each move is checked after. Empty folders stays the PC's: a phone has no Recycle Bin.",
    "- **Cover searches find more:** an album's name is searched for without its kind and signs (\"Lone Survivor - EP\", \"Talk (Single)\", slashes and dashes), in Pick a cover, Songs without covers and Wrapped's covers alike. Searches with them in found nothing.",
    "- **Tags match finds a song named a little differently:** after the recording code, the title and artist, and the file's name, a song is also found by a name only a little different: a word more or less, \"The Dirty Heads\" and \"Dirty Heads\", \"Dont\" and \"Don't\", an artist written twice (\"K Flay K Flay\"). Its length must be within a second, it must be the same kind of version (a rough, demo, snippet, live, remix or v2 is never the song itself), and exactly one song may fit. Anything less certain is left out of the sync, never guessed.",
    "- **Plays everywhere:** every song in every list (My music, playlists, albums, artist pages, search, the queue…) shows how often you've played it, beside its length, and every album, EP and single in those tabs shows its plays. So do playlists, smart playlists, genres, folders and moods, in the list and the grid. Counted as the Artists tab counts them: Last.fm and your imports, the plays here only without Last.fm. A song you haven't played shows none.",
    "- **How much you have, and how much you'd still only stream:** each album and EP says how many of its songs are on this device and how many you haven't downloaded (\"12 on this device · 2 not downloaded\"), from its tracklist: counted in the background a few at a time, and when you open it. An artist's page says the same over Every song (each song once, however many releases it is on; the ones you hid off the page not counted), and the Artists tab shows it for each artist whose page you've opened. Your unreleased music is left out of it: no count, and no missing songs looked for.",
    "",
    "## 4.0.35 · November 2026",
    "- **What damaged the files, found:** damaged songs taken from a phone and compared with good copies showed each to be a patchwork of two versions of itself, 4,000 bytes apart: two tag writes at work on the same file at once, one of them moving the music along inside it. Since 4.0.32 only one write to a song file happens at a time, and each is made on a copy that then replaces the file in one pass; this release adds the two guards below. Before it, the writers were run on copies of real songs 240 at a time, and the app was killed in the middle of writes 200 times: the music came back exactly as it was every time.",
    "- **A song's file is never changed while it plays:** no scan, tagger or cover write touches the file of the song playing or the next one. A scan leaves it for another time. A change you make yourself is kept and goes into the file a moment after the song has finished (or when the app next starts); a note says it is waiting.",
    "- **A song being written isn't played half-written:** a song whose file is being put down at the very moment it should play is given five seconds; if the write hasn't ended, the song is passed over and the next one plays, without asking. A note says why, and it is kept in Settings › Problems (nothing is wrong with the file).",
    "- **Hear the rest of an album before you download it:** the songs in Missing from this album (or EP) are found on your streaming service and listed like any streamed song: tap one to play it, queue it, add it to a playlist, pick several. Beside Download missing tracks, **Listen later** puts them all aside.",
    "- **Listen later: the album's songs I don't have:** with a song or an album picked anywhere, this new option (under Add to Listen later playlist) puts only the songs of its album you haven't got on Listen later. Add to Listen later playlist still takes every song picked.",
    "- **Listen later › Review:** lists the songs on it you haven't got, each to download, leave as a streaming song, or remove. A small Discovery of one album.",
    "- **The damaged list keeps up:** a song leaves it as soon as its new copy is downloaded, so what is left is what still needs replacing. **Check these again** reads only the listed files once more (seconds, not a full check): one that is whole now, because you put a good copy in its place yourself, leaves the list too.",
    "- **Sync makes the other device's songs exactly the main device's (Tags match):** pick the main device, and at each sync the other device's copy of each song is made to say what the main device's says: title, artist, album, album artist, genre, year, track, the lyrics and their mood, the moods (in the file and in the app, extra ones taken off) and the cover. What differs is replaced and what the main device's copy hasn't got is taken off; what is the same already isn't written again.",
    "- **A failsafe in that:** something is only taken off when the main device read its copy for certain and it truly has none. A file it couldn't read, or moods or a cover it couldn't tell, leaves the other device's as they are; a song whose name couldn't be read isn't changed at all, and a title or artist is never taken off.",
    "- **One Sync does it all:** when there are more covers than fit in one go, the sync carries on by itself, part after part, until every one has gone across.",
    "- **A cover you pick goes to your other device** at the next Sync, as your tag edits do.",
    "- **Low song count albums** holds only the albums you have one to four songs of; five or more are in Albums.",
    "- **Covers in Wrapped:** a single the app downloaded showed its artist's picture (or nothing) in place of its cover: its own file's cover is used now, and a name is also looked up without its featured artist. And any song, album, single or EP in Wrapped can have its cover picked by hand: right-click it (long-press on the phone) and choose one found on Google, Genius or Wikipedia, a picture of the artist, or a picture of yours. It is kept for that row from then on, and put into your own file of it when it is yours.",
    "- **Play and pause from the desktop's media controls (Linux):** the app told the desktop \"can't play, can't pause\" for the moment it started with no song, and never said otherwise, so a media menu that went by that left the play button doing nothing (next and previous worked). It now says so whenever a song comes or goes.",
    "",
    "## 4.0.34 · November 2026",
    "- **Full check finds every damaged place:** it carried on to the next file at the first fault; now it goes on through the file and notes each place (\"damaged in 3 places (about 0:20, 1:31 and 3:28 in)\"). FLACs and MP3s. Run Full check again to see them.",
    "- **Listen:** each song in the damaged list has a Listen button that plays it from a few seconds before where its damage is, to hear it for yourself. A song damaged in several places has one for each (A, B, C…, with its time). It plays as a queue of its own, as when you tap any song.",
    "- **File type (the ☰ menu):** a new section with a tab for each kind of file your songs are: FLACs, 24-bit FLACs and 32-bit FLACs (moved here from Music), MP3s, M4As and AACs, WAVs, Ogg and Opus, Other files. Each lists every song of that kind, with sorting and the list or grid layouts, for when one kind of file has a fault or needs tagging.",
    "- **Never show again goes over with Sync:** the damaged files you told the check to leave out are known by the song (title, artist and album) in place of where the file is, so they are left out on your other device too. What 4.0.33 kept is taken over at the next check.",
    "- **The strip of letters down the lists is taken out:** it made long lists slow to scroll, 24-bit FLACs most of all.",
    "- **Tag writes, proved on real files:** before this release every tag writer of the app's (lyrics, moods, the Tagger's fields, covers small and of megabytes, moods erased in place) was run on copies of 18 real songs (FLAC, MP3, WAV, M4A, some damaged on purpose), one after another and 240 at once from six threads. After each of 198 steps the copy's bytes of music were exactly what they had been.",
    "",
    "## 4.0.33 · November 2026",
    "- **Choose what to do with damaged files:** Storage and damaged files (Settings › Scanner › Your files) now opens a list of every damaged song, each with a tick box, none ticked to start with. Nothing is downloaded or replaced unless you tick it.",
    "- **Show replacements:** looks each ticked song up and downloads nothing. Under each song it shows the very song it would be replaced with: title, artist, album, length, quality, and whether it is the explicit version. What is different from yours is in red (another title, artist or album, how many seconds longer or shorter). A song that can't be found says so and is left as it is.",
    "- **Download new copies gets exactly what was shown:** only the ticked songs, each replaced with the song shown under it, with no second search. A file of your own still goes to the bin only after its new copy is here and you say yes.",
    "- **Never show again:** keeps the ticked files as they are and leaves them out of every later check, for a song you can't get again (one that was never released). Check them again, beside the check, undoes it. Kept on each device.",
    "",
    "## 4.0.32 · November 2026",
    "- **A tag write can't shorten or damage a song:** the lyrics and moods scans rewrote an MP3 whole, and two of them on the same file at once could write over each other, leaving a song that kept its size and played for 10 to 30 seconds. Every tag and cover write of the app's (the scans, the Tagger, covers, repairs, on the phone and the PC) now takes its turn, one file at a time; the change is made on a copy, and a copy holding less music than the song had is thrown away, your file untouched. After a write the file is measured, and written again if not all of it landed.",
    "- **Full check:** Storage and damaged files (Settings › Scanner › Your files) has a second button that reads every song file from start to end and checks the sound itself: a FLAC's frames by their checksums and its length against what it says it holds, an MP3's frames one after another (a stretch that isn't music is found, with about where), an Ogg's or Opus's pages by their checksums, a WAV's and an M4A's parts against the file. It takes a long time (it reads your whole library) and can be stopped; what it found so far is shown. An MP3 cut off cleanly at its very end, and damage inside a WAV's or M4A's sound, can't be told. The quick Check is as it was.",
    "- **The quick Check finds more:** an MP3 or WAV that plays for far less time than the music it holds is listed as damaged.",
    "- **Stutter, static and cut-outs in hi-res FLACs (the phone):** the decoder was given room for 32 KB of a song at a time, and a bigger piece was thrown away (Problems: \"Buffer too small\", then \"timestamp discontinuity\"). It now has room for the biggest piece a FLAC can have. If the file itself is damaged at that spot a click may remain there: Full check lists such a file.",
    "- **Problems is for faults:** another app taking the sound, or the car or headphones switching off, stops the music as it should and is no longer written in Settings › Problems.",
    "",
    "## 4.0.31 · November 2026",
    "- **Download new copies of damaged files:** Storage and damaged files (Settings › Scanner › Your files) has **Download new copies** under its list of damaged files. A song the app downloaded is got again the way it first was, and its damaged file removed only once the new one is saved. A file of your own is got from Tidal in FLAC (Tidal connected), and the damaged file goes to the bin only after its new copy is here and you say yes. Playlists, favorites and plays stay with each song; a song that can't be found again is left as it is.",
    "- **The check finds more:** a FLAC whose tags and cover no longer lead to its music (what a tag write that was cut short leaves) is listed as damaged. Its first bytes look right, so the check missed it before.",
    "- **A tag write can't leave half a file:** writing tags, lyrics, moods or a cover into a song now writes a whole copy first, checks that the copy still holds the music, and only then puts it in the song's place. A write that is interrupted (the app closed, the phone switched off) is finished the next time the app starts.",
    "- **A damaged FLAC is put right when it's played (the phone):** a FLAC that fails with \"PARSING_CONTAINER_MALFORMED\" is looked through for where its music starts and its tags rebuilt around it, then played. A copy of the file as it was is kept. A file too damaged for that is left as it is: Download new copies replaces it.",
    "",
    "## 4.0.30 · November 2026",
    "- **What counts as a skip:** pressing Next (the button, the headphones, the notification) with more than 15 seconds of the song left, and nothing else. Picking another song from a list, the app moving on by itself, and going back are not skips. Before, every change of song but Previous counted, so the songs you play most led Most skipped.",
    "- **A clean slate for the app's own skips:** they are counted from 21 November 2026. Skips counted before are left in place and not counted (so a Sync can't bring them back); songs heard to the end are counted as ever.",
    "- **Spotify's skips, more strictly:** only the forward button is a skip (Spotify's own mark also covers going back and picking another song). Import your Spotify zip once more and its skips are redone.",
    "- **The Skips list is in the right order:** two counted songs that are one file of yours showed as two rows with one's count, so a row sorted as 100 skips said 1. They are one row, added up.",
    "- **Where your music came from counts by where you took the song:** a song put in the queue from a playlist, an album, an artist's page or search counts for that place when it plays. Radio and ∞ count only the songs they themselves added. From now on.",
    "- **Milestones are in All-time Wrapped only, and match your plays:** they are worked out from the same plays the rest of that Wrapped shows. A week's, a month's and a year's Wrapped have no Milestones page.",
    "- **This app's own play history, out and in:** Settings › Accounts (with the imports) has Export and Import for what Radon Player counted itself: its plays (the fallback for when Last.fm isn't connected, and Recently played), the songs you skipped or heard out, and its listening diary. Export writes one file; Import on another device or another install of the app adds what that one hasn't got. Importing the same file twice adds nothing.",
    "- **A song is not rewritten while it plays:** the scans that write tags by themselves (lyrics, moods, covers from folders) leave the song playing and the next one alone; a tag write puts the whole file down again, and a song opened at that moment read as broken (\"Couldn't play: PARSING_CONTAINER_MALFORMED\"). A file that reads as broken is also tried once more after two seconds.",
    "- **Covers on Wrapped's Never skipped and Most skipped:** looked up for songs that aren't yours, as Most played's are.",
    "",
    "## 4.0.29 · November 2026",
    "- **The cause of music going silent while the seek bar moved (the phone):** found and fixed. When the sound output had to finish what it held (at a song's end, and in the middle of a song whenever a fraction of a second went missing from the decoder), two of the app's own sound steps, the resampler and the peak repair, gave it a little more silence every time they were asked, where they should have answered once. The output never finished, and was fed silence without end while its clock ran on, until the song was skipped. Both answer once now, so after such a gap the song carries on.",
    "- **Android's own FLAC decoder (the phone):** Settings › Sound quality has a switch to decode FLACs with Android's own decoder in place of the phone maker's. The maker's was seen dropping a fraction of a second of a song now and then (the stutter). Off at first: turn it on if you still hear short skips, and off again if songs sound wrong with it.",
    "- **Wrapped asks which one you want:** opening Wrapped shows This week, This month, This year and All time, and puts together only the one you tap. It used to start loading the year at once.",
    "- **The time you picked is the one shown:** All time (or any other) loaded, then was replaced a moment later by the year that had been loading behind it. A result is shown only for the time still asked for.",
    "",
    "## 4.0.28 · November 2026",
    "- **Wrapped's summary card:** your top artists and top songs each show their plays, as your top obsessions show their repeats. On a phone held upright the three lists go one under another across the card's whole width, so every song, artist and count is shown whole.",
    "- **Most skipped and Never skipped are top 15s.**",
    "- **Your playlists are counted:** a play from one of your playlists was written down under the tab's name, so Wrapped said you had played none. It is the playlist's now, and the plays already written down are put right (a sitting is the playlist most of its songs are in).",
    "- **A song picked on an artist's or album's page is not radio:** those pages start a radio after their list, and the songs you picked yourself were counted as radio. Past plays are put right too.",
    "- **Searching and queueing counts as a search:** a song found by searching and put in the queue (or Play next) counts toward Most searched and toward Search in Where your music came from, as a song tapped does. From now on.",
    "- **Skips from your Spotify history:** the extended streaming history says which plays you skipped and which you heard out. Import the zip once more and they are added to the Skips tab, to Wrapped's Most skipped and Never skipped, and to what the DJ knows. Plays you have already are not doubled.",
    "- **An Apple Music library's undated counts in All-time Wrapped:** songs, artists, albums, plays and minutes. Nothing that needs a day can use them.",
    "- **Every source in every count:** Wrapped's compared-with-before now counts your imports on both sides; the week, month and year lists and the listening times take the plays in this app when Last.fm isn't connected.",
    "- **Another app taking the sound for a moment is not written to Problems:** voice typing, a call or a spoken direction makes the music wait and come back, as it should. It is kept in Details for support.",
    "",
    "## 4.0.27 · November 2026",
    "- **A stutter leaves a line in Settings › Problems (the phone):** the sound output running dry, being made again mid-song, the player jumping by itself, or another app taking the sound for a moment is noted there with the song, the place in it and what happened just before (once every 20 seconds at most). Before, these were only in Details for support, kept while the app ran.",
    "- **The fault behind the music cutting out, caught at its first step (the phone):** the player sometimes moves on to the next piece of a song while the phone's sound output is still part-way through the last. The output then either refuses the new piece (the player stopped: silence with the seek bar moving) or miscounts (a stutter, a jump in the song). The app now sees that moment, has the output let go of the leftover few thousandths of a second, and carries on, before the output sees anything wrong. Noted in Settings › Problems each time, with how far it jumped.",
    "- **A song that plays on with nothing reaching the sound output is started again in place:** four seconds of the output taking no music while the player says it plays (seen once after a recovery: forty seconds of nothing): a seek in place, then a full reload, three times a song at most.",
    "- **The decoder is logged:** made, let go or failing, and one made again in the middle of a song is noted in Problems.",
    "- **Wrapped's summary lists your top 15 obsessions:** beside your top artists and top songs, your most repeated songs, each with how often it was repeated (the count first, so it is never cut off).",
    "- **Obsession of each month shows the artist and the repeats:** under each month's song, whose it is and how many times it was repeated that month.",
    "- **Wrapped's minutes and the listening times were far too big with an Apple Music history:** a day's count in it carries the whole day where a play has its length, and that was added up as listening (all-time Wrapped said 14 million minutes). Those plays count the song's own length now. Most played's listening times are added up again.",
    "- **Obsessions are named as your own files are:** a song your history has under somebody else's name shows its real artist in Wrapped's repeats pages.",
    "",
    "## 4.0.26 · November 2026",
    "- **Music that went silent partway while the seek bar kept moving (the phone):** the player itself was stopping: the phone's sound output refused a piece of the song it was handed (Settings › Problems showed FAILED_RUNTIME_CHECK), and the app's clock counted on with nothing playing, again each time the song was loaded. The app now makes the output let go of what it was holding and hands the music in again, so the song carries on. Each time is noted in Settings › Problems.",
    "- **A song that still won't keep playing moves on:** after being loaded again twice it goes on to the next song (or shows as paused, with nothing after it), not silence until you skip it.",
    "- **It stays the phone's music player while it recovers:** a stopped song about to be loaded again no longer makes Android let the app go, after which it was refused (\"startForegroundService() not allowed\") with the screen off.",
    "- **Every song is watched for going silent:** the watch for a song playing on with no sound only looked at hi-res music, and not at all with the equalizer doing nothing. MP3s, streams and CD-quality files are watched too, and started again in place.",
    "- **Details for support says more about the sound:** what the sound output did lately (set up, let go, ran dry, failed, another app taking the sound) and when real sound last left the app.",
    "- **The song the DJ names plays first:** a set's line was written before its songs were put in order, so a song said to lead could come second. The first song the line names is moved to the front.",
    "- **Duplicates finds a download saved with no length:** a song downloaded from a list without lengths (Repeats, your history) showed no time and could not be matched to your other file of it. Its length is read from the file.",
    "",
    "## 4.0.25 · November 2026",
    "- **Your file under another of its artists:** a phone reads only the first artist of a file that credits two, so a song your history has under the other one showed as a stream in Repeats, and downloading it made a second copy. The song's length is now taken from your imported Spotify history as well as Apple Music's, and your only song of that name and that length is taken to be it.",
    "- **Duplicates finds one song tagged two ways:** your files of the same name by an artist both credit, or of the same name and the very same length whoever each names, are listed together. A group put together by name and length alone has no copy marked Remove until you mark one, with a note to play both first: two different songs of one name can run to the same length.",
    "",
    "## 4.0.24 · November 2026",
    "- **Hide from stats:** long-press (right-click on a computer) a song, an album or an artist in a stats list and pick Hide from stats. It is no longer shown in Most played (songs, albums, artists and their listening times), Repeats, Skips or Unplayed and neglected; its plays still count in those lists' totals. Wrapped leaves it out altogether. Settings › Scanner › Last.fm & your stats › Hidden from stats lists what is hidden, with Show again. The list goes to your other device at a Sync.",
    "- **Streaming or on this device:** every song in every list says where it plays from. A song of yours shows its format (or Local until the file has been read); a song that isn't on this device shows Streaming, with a small cloud on its cover where songs are shown as covers.",
    "- **Covers found more often:** a song nothing was found for was not looked for again for two weeks. Every earlier miss is now looked for again once, a miss is tried again after three days, and a service that couldn't be reached after ten minutes. The search asks Deezer first and plainly, and Genius last, for the songs no shop sells.",
    "",
    "## 4.0.23 · November 2026",
    "- **A song your history has under somebody else's name is your file:** an unreleased song is often put up under an uploader's name, so your history had it under an artist who isn't on your file. Your only song of the same name and the same length (to the second) is now taken to be it: it shows with your file's artist, cover and tags, and its plays count for that artist in Most played, Artists and Wrapped. What plays from a service is never swapped this way.",
    "- **This is a song of mine…:** for a song whose name is different too. A song's Options lets you pick which of your files it is, once (your songs of the same length are listed first); it is then shown, played and counted as that file. Your file's Options undoes it. A file with no artist in its tags is not accepted: give it one first.",
    "- **WAVs keep their tags on the phone:** Android reads no tags from a WAV (its name came from the file, its album from the folder, its artist was unknown), so a WAV you tagged went back to Unknown artist at the next scan. The app now reads each WAV's own title, artist and album from the file.",
    "- **Covers in Repeats, Recently played and Listen later:** a song that isn't yours and came with no picture has its cover looked up, as Most played does; when nothing has its cover, its artist's picture stands in.",
    "",
    "## 4.0.22 · November 2026",
    "- **Music that went silent partway through a song (the phone):** anything outside the app that talks to its player (the earbuds, a watch, the lock screen, the system) could tell that player to Stop, or to empty or replace what it held. The song then went silent while the app still took it for playing, and Play started it from its beginning. From outside, Stop is now a Pause and the song in the player is left alone. Each such request is noted in Settings › Problems.",
    "- **Two safety nets for the same:** a player found idle with its song still meant to be playing is loaded again where it was (at most twice a song), and Play carries on from where the song was, not from its start. A volume still turned down after half a minute with the DJ not talking is put back to full, whatever was holding it.",
    "- **No pop-up for what the DJ said, or for the equalizer changing with the output:** both are gone (the DJ is heard, and the Equalizer shows which one is on).",
    "- **Messages are not played back late:** opening the app after a while showed every message made since, one after another. Only the newest waits, and one made more than a few seconds ago is not shown.",
    "- **Details for support says more about the sound:** the output the music is on, the player's volume, and the last few things that changed (with their times), for finding what happened when music goes quiet.",
    "",
    "## 4.0.21 · November 2026",
    "- **The DJ has more than one way to say each thing:** the things it tells you between songs (about a song, an artist, your listening, your collection, your repeats) had one sentence each, so a set that came round again brought the same words. 74 of those lines now take turns with one or two others that say the same: 90 new wordings in all. None is said again until the others have had their turn, and it carries on from where it was after the app is closed. Introducing a set already had several wordings.",
    "- **A song you used to repeat:** one of \"You used to have this one on repeat, a long time ago. You haven't in a while.\", \"You wore this one out once. Not lately.\" and \"This one used to be on repeat for you. It's been a while.\"",
    "",
    "## 4.0.20 · November 2026",
    "- **Other device's songs: the same song however each device names it:** songs both devices have were listed as missing when one of them was a file with no tags, because each device names such a file in its own way. They are now matched when one calls it \"Artist - Title\" and the other \"Title\" (or \"Title - Artist\"), with underscores in a name or without, with a track number in front (\"11 Ruby\") or not, with no artist on one side, and with a \$ for an s (\"\$uicideboy\$\", \"Suicideboys\"). A song the other device really has and this one lacks is still listed.",
    "- **A file with no artist, or named with its track number, is your song:** such a file (\"11 Ruby\", no artist) was not taken as your copy of a song named anywhere else (Repeats, Most played, your history), so it showed with no cover and as not on the device. It is now found without the number and, with no artist in its tags, by its name alone.",
    "- **Linux: the close button on desktops with no tray and no minimized windows** (Hyprland, Sway and the like): the app answered Close by asking to be minimized, which those desktops don't do, so the button did nothing. There the window is now hidden while music plays (it goes on, the desktop's media controls still work, and opening the app again shows it) and the app closes when nothing is playing. A note says so the first time.",
    "- **Size of everything is each device's own:** it went across at Sync, so the phone's size landed on the PC. It is no longer sent or taken by Sync, and a backup put on the other device leaves its size alone, so a device is at 100% until you change it there. Favorite settings, hidden choices and preset edits go across as before.",
    "- **The DJ on a song you used to repeat:** \"You used to have this one on repeat, a long time ago. You haven't in a while.\" (it named the month it was last on repeat).",
    "- **Software drawing is gone from Settings:** it wasn't used. Where a window flickers or stays black, starting the app once with RADON_RENDER=software still draws it without the graphics card.",
    "",
    "## 4.0.19 · November 2026",
    "- **Your own songs are recognised in every list:** a list made from your history, or kept from before a song was downloaded, moved or tagged again, could show a song you have with no cover and as not on the device. A song is now found by what it is (the same download or file) first, then by its name and artist, then under any other artist it is credited to (history says \"hard life & BENEE\", your file says \"BENEE\": the same song), and is shown as it stands today: its cover (a WAV's too), length, format and tags. Repeats, its collage and the DJ's repeat sets use it for every song.",
    "- **Now playing shows your file as it is:** a song played from your own copy out of such a list went on showing the old names, no cover and \"streaming\". The song playing is now your copy as it stands today.",
    "- **Other device's songs works:** at each Sync the phone and the PC sent each other the list of their songs, and both dropped it on arrival, so Settings › Remote & sync › Other device's songs never had anything to show. It is kept now, and the first few missing songs are listed right under the setting (Review… has them all, to tick and download). Both devices need this version; then tap Sync now once.",
    "- **Linux: Update works for a copy in your own folders:** on a Linux with no .deb installer (Arch, Fedora and the like) the app is unpacked into the home folder, and Update only downloaded a package nothing could install. Such a copy now updates itself: the new version is unpacked beside it, swapped in as the app closes and started, and the one before is kept once. A copy installed by the package manager updates as before.",
    "",
    "## 4.0.18 · November 2026",
    "- **Jump to a letter, bigger and on every long list:** the strip of letters is as tall as the list, wide enough for a thumb, and its letters are as big as fit (it was too small to read or hit on a phone); the rows keep clear of it. Every list of 20 or more has one now: songs anywhere, albums, artists, playlists, smart playlists, moods, pins, Most played's tabs, an artist's albums, New releases and Discovery. In a list in no order by name (by plays, by date, your own order) a letter goes to the first name that starts with it.",
    "- **Size of everything (Settings › General):** makes the app's text and everything else bigger or smaller together, from 80% to 250%, for a screen where it all comes out too small or too big. On the PC it changes at once; on the phone the screen is drawn again. The PC's mini player keeps its usual size.",
    "- **Play on the PC stops the phone's own song:** switching Play on (or pairing) left the phone's song playing under the PC's, with every button going to the PC. The phone now pauses what it was playing; its song and queue are kept for when you switch back.",
    "- **Pinwheel visualizer:** it jumped about, more wildly the longer it had been running (the Fruit stand and Fairground presets use it). It now turns steadily, faster with the bass.",
    "- **Linux, on Hyprland, Sway, niri, i3 and the like:** the app was drawn at the size it asked for in a corner of a bigger window, with a line round it. On those desktops it now starts itself with the setting that makes it follow its window. Other desktops and Windows are unchanged.",
    "",
    "## 4.0.17 · November 2026",
    "- **Lyrics card: tick the first line and the last:** the first line you tick is marked **1**; tick another and it is marked **2** and every line between them is ticked too (upward as well), so a verse is two taps. A ticked line unticks on its own, a third line starts another run, and **Clear** starts over. Still up to 25 lines; after a fill the card shows the lines in the song's order.",
    "- **The Lyrics card box scrolls:** the list of lines keeps its full height under the card and the sliders (it had shrunk to two lines on a phone with the sound ticked). Scroll down past the card to reach the lines.",
    "- **A clip is kept while its box is open:** once a clip is made (a lyrics card with sound, or Share a clip with sound) it goes to Instagram, then Facebook, then Save or Messages without being made again. Changing the clip makes a new one, and closing the box throws the kept one away (a saved copy stays).",
    "- **Hear it and Start where it's playing start a paused song:** with the song paused they moved it without playing it.",
    "- **Repeats from Apple Music:** Apple's Play Activity file often leaves the artist out of its rows, and those rows were all skipped, so no repeats were found from an Apple download that had the file. They are now kept for Repeats, the artist found by the song's title from the download's other files. **Import the zip again** to read them; your plays are not counted twice.",
    "- **Jump to a letter on more lists:** the strip of letters now follows what a list is sorted by: songs sorted by artist or album get the artists' or albums' letters, a list from Z to A gets its strip the other way up, and playlists by name have one. A list in another order (by plays, by date) has none: letters would mean nothing there.",
    "- **New releases: a bell, not a notification:** what the app's daily look finds (Settings › Playback › New releases by themselves) goes under a bell on the New releases screen, with a count of what is new; no notification is sent. It looks at the artists of the songs you have as files or downloads and of the songs in your Radon Player playlists; a playlist that is only on a streaming service doesn't count.",
    "- **Unplayed and neglected, a new tab (Statistics):** the songs of yours you have never played, and the ones not played in 3 months, 6 months, a year or 2 years, the longest ago first, each with when it was last played and its plays; Play or Shuffle them from there. It goes by your stats (Last.fm and your imports) and the plays here.",
    "- **Shuffle, three ways (Settings › Playback › Shuffle):** the Smart shuffle switch is now a choice. **Random** is pure chance; **Smart** is the old switch; **Unheard and neglected** plays only songs you haven't heard in a long time or at all, the never played first, then the ones heard longest ago, loosely shuffled. A song you have heard is left out of every shuffle, of any list, until the rest of that list has been heard too, then it goes round again; it remembers across queues and restarts, so a shuffled list can be shorter than the list it came from. A song you skip isn't counted as heard.",
    "- **Mood lists is gone from the menu and the bar:** the Moods tab already opens each mood's songs. Nothing kept was deleted.",
    "",
    "## 4.0.16 · November 2026",
    "- **Select several and Hide, in every Personalization menu:** each menu (Look, Theme, Layout, Effects, the visualizer menus, the colour blends, Wrapped style, Lyrics card style, Presets) starts with **Select several**: tick as many as you like, then **Hide** them, **Copy codes**, or (presets) **Edit**. Hidden ones leave the menu and are listed under **Hidden** at its end: a tap brings one back, and \"Show all of them again\" brings them all. The one in use is never hidden while it is in use. Hide is also on each choice's own little menu (hold it, its ⋯, or right-click).",
    "- **Edit the ready-made presets:** hold a ready-made preset (or tick several) and tap **Edit**: take any of its effects off (the others stay), add effects, and set Now playing's middle visualizer and its bottom one each to as it came, None, or another style. Its look, theme, layout and the rest stay as they came. Nothing ready-made is ever deleted; **Reset default preset edits**, at the end of every Personalization menu, puts them all back as they came. (Follows the cover and Glass cover take their effects and visualizers from each song, so they have none to edit.)",
    "- **Share anything from Personalization as a code:** hold any choice in any Personalization menu and tap **Copy code** (or tick several and Copy codes): one line of text to send to someone or to your other device. Something you made goes as it is; a preset goes whole, with the look, layout, visualizers, colours, Wrapped style and lyrics card it uses when those are yours; a ready-made preset goes with your edits and arrives as a preset of theirs; any other ready-made choice goes by name and is put on when pasted. Settings › Personalization › **Share** has the box to paste a code into, and **Copy everything on now** and **Copy my effects**. Pasting adds to what you have, never replaces it. Until now only a look you had made yourself had a code, which is why most people saw only the paste box.",
    "- **Clips with sound from streamed songs:** Share a clip with sound, and the lyrics card's With the song's sound, now work for a song you only stream: its sound is taken from the stream when you save or share (it needs the internet and takes a little longer), and its cover is fetched for the card. Tidal's hi-res comes in pieces a clip can't be cut from, so for the clip CD quality is asked for. A stream that only exists in pieces says it can't be clipped.",
    "- **Sound on any lyrics card:** With the song's sound can be ticked for every song. A song whose words came from its own file (or the lyrics scanner) was shown without times, so the card said it had no timed lyrics: they are now looked up when you tick it. With timed lyrics the sound follows the lines you tick; **Starts** and **Ends** sliders are there to set it yourself (Back to the lines undoes that). When nobody has the lyrics timed, you set where the sound starts and ends.",
    "- **Favorite settings:** the first section of Settings. Tap the ☆ beside any setting's name and it shows there too, for the ones you use all the time (Auto mono, say); it stays in its own section as well, and it is the same setting, not a copy. Tap ★ to take it out. Only the setting's own line shows there: extra rows some settings have under them stay in their section.",
    "- **Fetch the next song ahead (Settings › Playback, on):** for songs you stream. While a song plays, the one after it in the queue is fetched whole into the app's cache and played from there, so it starts with no wait and can't stall halfway. Until now only its address was looked up ahead. Tidal's hi-res is put together as one FLAC, as a download is. Nothing is kept. It uses the same data as playing the song, plus a song's worth when you skip one that was fetched: switch it off on a small data plan. Not used while casting.",
    "- **Repeats tab:** a song's line is its number alone (\"#1 · 164\"), so it is no longer cut short on a tile; and **Collage…** makes the grid of covers as on Most played, each with its repeats, for the time the tab shows.",
    "",
    "## 4.0.15 · November 2026",
    "- **Share a clip with sound:** a song's Options has **Share a clip with sound…**: a short video (.mp4) of a card with the song's cover, its name and artist and a seek bar that fills as it plays, with the song itself playing, so it plays sound wherever you post it. Two sliders set where it starts and ends, from 3 seconds to a minute; if the song is playing, \"Start where it's playing\" and \"Hear it from there\" help find the spot. The card comes in every lyrics card style. Save it or share it; the phone has Instagram and Facebook buttons, and on the PC Share saves it to Downloads › Radon Player and copies the file to paste into a message. Only a song you have as a file (yours, or a download) can be clipped: a stream has no file to take the sound from.",
    "- **Lyrics cards with sound:** the Lyrics card box has a new tick, **With the song's sound**. With it on the card is shared as a short video with the song playing, from the first line you tick to where the last one stops (no seek bar on it). It needs the song's timed lyrics and its file; without them the card is a picture as before.",
    "- **Clips in Personalization:** Song clip style, Song clip length (15, 30, 45 or 60 seconds to begin with) and Lyrics cards with sound, after Lyrics card style.",
    "- **Plays on cards:** lyrics cards, picture cards and song clips say how often you have played the song beside the artist (\"Saint Motel · 132 plays\"). Personalization › Plays on cards switches it off.",
    "- **Repeats, a new tab:** each time a song plays to its end and starts over by itself with **Repeat one** on is counted as one repeat of it (Repeat all, and picking a song again yourself, don't count). The **Repeats** tab (Statistics in the menu) lists your songs by how often you let them play again, with each one's longest run in a row, for all time, the last year, 30 days or 7 days.",
    "- **Repeats from before, and from other services:** nothing recorded repeats before this version, and no service says a song was on Repeat one. So the app looks through what you played here before, your Last.fm scrobbles and your imported history (Spotify, Deezer, YouTube Music, Tidal, and Apple Music's Play Activity file) for a song played again the moment it ended, and takes each for a repeat. It does this by itself after an import, when Last.fm is connected, and once after this update; what it finds is kept and not looked for again until your history changes. It is a good guess, not a record. Apple's daily counts and a library exported from the Music app have no exact times, so no repeats can be found from those; Settings says so beside the imports.",
    "- **The DJ knows your repeats:** with the DJ on and a song on Repeat one it says so as the song starts over: at three and five times in a row, at its 10th, 25th, 50th, 100th… repeat, when a run passes the song's longest ever, and after a long run it offers to carry on with something else (the Repeats tab has a switch for that). Four new sets: **Can't let go** (your most repeated songs), **This month's obsessions**, **Old obsessions** (what you had on repeat about a year ago) and **Like your repeats** (songs close to the ones you repeat most). Before a song it may say how often you've had it on repeat, your record with it, how long since, or that its artist is the one you repeat most. With the DJ off, a milestone or a new longest run shows as a short note (a switch in the Repeats tab).",
    "- **Repeats in Wrapped:** every Wrapped (a week, a month, a year, all time) has your repeats in it when there were any: how many and how long they add up to, your biggest obsession, your most repeated songs and artists as full lists (as long as the other lists), your longest run, your repeat day, the day of the week and time of day you repeat most, the song you repeated most each month, and the kind of repeater you are.",
    "- **\"Repeat\" is now \"On repeat\":** the chart of what you played most lately keeps its place and is called On repeat (as the DJ calls it), so it isn't mistaken for the new Repeats tab.",
    "- **Presets can leave your visualizers alone:** under Personalization Preset Shuffle, **Presets leave my middle visualizer** and **Presets leave my bottom visualizer**. With both on, presets bring only their looks; with one, you get a preset's other visualizer with your own. They go for presets you pick, the shuffle, presets by time of day and Follows the cover.",
    "- **Now playing in the cover's colours:** with the Now playing look, the Now playing screen was black while every other screen took the cover's colours. It takes them now, as do the small player window, the lock-screen player and the lyrics card.",
    "- **Jump to a letter shows:** the strip of letters down a long A to Z list never appeared. It does now, and a few names out of place (a title filed under its bracket, an accented name after Z) no longer stop it.",
    "- **Details for support:** the line says how many problems are in it, and **Clear** empties them once they are fixed.",
    "- **Gone:** the switch Blurred cover behind everything (Personalization › Effects). The Glass cover preset still turns the blurred cover on.",
    "",
    "## 4.0.14 · November 2026",
    "- **Thirty more of everything in Personalization:** 30 new looks, 30 new themes, 30 new layouts, 30 new effects, 30 new visualizers, 30 new visualizer colour blends, 30 new Wrapped styles, 30 new lyrics cards, and 30 new presets that put them together. None is a recolour of what was there. The new looks are after materials and places (Stained glass, Copper patina, Blueprint, Terrazzo, Art deco, Bauhaus, Jade, Coral reef, Ink wash, Denim, Velvet theatre, Glacier cave, Origami, Marble hall, Peacock, Lighthouse, Tea house, Crayon box, Lantern festival, Game cartridge, Opal, Kite sky and more), each with an effect drawn for it (panes of glass, drafting lines, deco fans, paper cranes, bunting, lanterns, kites…). The new visualizers are things: organ pipes, an abacus, piano keys, stalactites, pennants, a rose window, a compass, a lotus, an ink circle, a snow crystal, a gem, a pinwheel. Each new preset (Cathedral, Drawing office, Opening night, Tea ceremony, Player two, Windy day…) sets a look, its effect, a new theme and layout, two new visualizers in the look's own blend, and the Wrapped style and lyrics card made to match.",
    "- **Follows the cover, a preset that changes with every song:** the Now playing look already takes its colours from the cover of the song playing. This preset (first in the Presets menu) goes further: each cover picks a whole scene to match, the effects behind everything and Now playing's two visualizers, all drawn in the cover's colour. There are 17 scenes, so one is ready for any cover: twelve by colour round the colour wheel (Embers for red, Lantern light for orange, Fireflies for yellow, Meadow, Forest, Jungle pool, Reef for teal, Open sky, Rain for blue, Galaxy for violet, Dream for purple, Blossom for pink), and Noir, Silver, Mist, Night and Daylight for covers that are black-and-white, grey, very dark or very pale. The whole app's background becomes the cover as if blurred right out behind everything: its own colours running down the window (a cover that is teal above, red in the middle and navy below gives a window that is too), under the Glass theme's see-through cards. It leaves your layout alone, and keeps the effects and visualizers you picked yourself: they come back when you put another preset on or turn off **Scenes from the cover** (Personalization, above Effects), which says which scene is on now.",
    "- **Glass cover, a see-through preset:** with it on, the cover of the song playing is drawn blurred behind the whole app, every screen, washed in the cover's own colour, and everything sits over it see-through: the lists, cards, chips and bars are frosted panes you see the blurred cover through (it puts on the Frosted pills theme). It changes with every song. Your effects, visualizers and layout stay as they are. The switch is **Blurred cover behind everything** (Personalization, above Effects), which works with any theme; a see-through one (Glass, Frosted pills, Cut glass) shows it best.",
    "- **Details for support (Settings › General):** one Copy button for everything someone helping would ask: the version, the device, how many songs and playlists, the look and layout, sync, free space, and what Problems says. No sign-ins, folders or song names are in it.",
    "- **Storage and damaged files (Settings › Scanner › Your files):** one check that looks at every song file and says what takes the space, by kind of file, artist and album, with the biggest songs, as bars. It also lists files that are damaged in a way that shows without playing them: empty, not a music file at all, or far too small for the song's length (cut short while copying or downloading), each with its real name and the reason. Nothing is changed or deleted. A file that is whole but faulty somewhere inside can still only be found by playing it.",
    "- **What's new, after an update:** the first time the app opens after it has been updated, a card names what the update brought, with a button to read about them in the Changelog. Once for each version; never on a fresh install.",
    "- **Favorites sync by themselves too:** Auto playlist sync (Settings › Remote & sync) now carries your favorites as well as your synced playlists: star a song on the phone and it is starred on the PC within about 20 seconds while both apps are open, and the other way.",
    "- **A song as a picture card:** a song's Options has **Share as a picture card**: its cover, name and artist on a card in any of the lyrics card styles, with a line of your own if you like, to save or share as a picture. No link is made.",
    "- **Send an album or a few songs to your other device:** pick songs (or an album's), open Options and choose **Send to the PC** (or the phone): they arrive there as a playlist, named after the album when they are all from one. As with Send a playlist it is a copy made once, and any song the other device can't play is named.",
    "- **The phone edits the PC's queue:** with the phone as the PC's remote, the queue on the phone can now be reordered by dragging, cleared, and undone, as well as songs removed and added as before. Both need this version.",
    "- **What a sync changed:** under Sync in Settings, **What the last sync changed here** lists it in words: which playlists gained or lost songs (or are new, renamed or gone), how many favorites came and went, and which other things were brought up to date.",
    "- **On this day (Most played):** under Milestones, what you were listening to this week a year ago, two years ago and further back, from your listening history, each year with a Play button.",
    "- **Presets by time of day (Settings › Personalization):** pick a preset for the morning, the day, the evening and the night, and each is put on by itself when that part of the day begins (and when the app opens in it). A part left on No change keeps whatever is on.",
    "- **Back up by itself (Settings › Backup):** every day or every week the app saves a backup of its own accord while it is open, and keeps the newest five. Save a copy puts the newest wherever you choose; on the phone that matters, since the app's own folder goes when the app is uninstalled.",
    "- **Share a look you made:** under Make your own, a look of yours can be copied as a short code (a line of text starting RadonLook:) to send to someone or to your other device, and a code pasted there adds that look to yours.",
    "- **Shorten the ☰ menu (Settings › Personalization › Menu entries):** every entry of the menu has a switch; off, it is out of the menu. Nothing is removed from the app: a hidden screen can still be a button on your layout's bar, and comes back with its switch.",
    "- **A cover of your own for a playlist:** a playlist's menu has **Set a cover…**: pick a picture and it is shown for the playlist in place of its first song's cover (Remove its cover goes back). The cover is this device's own.",
    "- **Weekly mix:** Playlists has **Make this week's mix**: a playlist called Weekly mix made from what you have been playing lately with songs of yours you haven't heard in three months or more among them (two you know, then one to rediscover; up to 30 songs). Made again the same week it is the same mix; next week, a new one.",
    "- **Swipe a song (phone; Settings › Playback):** choose what dragging a song's row to the right and to the left does: add it to the queue, play it next, put it on Listen later, favorite it, or add it to a playlist. Both sides start on Nothing, so nothing changes until you choose. The PC has no swipes: its right-click menu has the same actions.",
    "- **New releases by themselves (Settings › Playback):** switched on, the app looks once a day (while it is open) at what the artists you have music by put out in the last week, and tells you when there is something new, on the phone with a notification. Each release is told once.",
    "- **Wake-up timer (Settings › Playback):** set a time and a playlist, and the app starts playing it then. It is a timer inside the app, not the phone's alarm clock: it only goes off while the app is running, so it suits a PC, or a phone left open on its stand; don't rely on it for something you can't be late for.",
    "- **Jump to a letter:** a long list in alphabetical order (songs, albums, artists, genres sorted by name) has a strip of letters down its right side: tap a letter, or run a finger down them, and the list goes to where that letter begins. A list in another order (by date, by plays) has no strip, since letters would mean nothing there.",
    "- **Concert dates on an artist's page:** with a Ticketmaster key of your own (free; Settings › Remote & sync › Concert dates says how to get one and is where you paste it), every artist's page has a **Concerts** section: their coming shows, soonest first, each with the venue, the city and a link to tickets. Add your city to see only shows there. Tribute acts and festivals that only mention the artist are left out. Only shows Ticketmaster sells are listed.",
    "- **Song radio:** a song's Options has **Song radio (songs like this)**: the song plays, and after it songs like it from your streaming service, as a queue of its own.",
    "- **Running out of room:** Settings › Your music says how much space is free where your downloads go, and a warning comes up when you start a download with under 1 GB (or 3%) left.",
    "- **See it before you put it on:** every Personalization menu shows each choice as several live screens to swipe through, not one picture. A preset shows Now playing with its visualizers moving, My music and Albums in its look, theme and layout with its effects behind them, three slides of Wrapped (top songs, top artist, your year) and its lyrics card. A look shows Now playing, My music, Albums and a Wrapped slide; a theme and a layout show the lists they change; an effect shows by itself, behind Now playing and behind My music, moving; a visualizer shows on Now playing and by itself; a Wrapped style shows three slides. The songs in them are samples.",
    "- **The Personalization menus fold into sections:** with this much to pick from, each menu now opens as a short list of sections, your ★ favourites first, with only the section of the one in use open: Looks by Basics, Light, Dark, Neon and Materials and places; Themes by what they change; Layouts by where the buttons are; Effects by Sky, Nature, Patterns, Light and Things; visualizers by where they stand. Everything is still there, a tap further in.",
    "- **Undo for the queue:** cleared the queue, shuffled it, dragged a song to the wrong place or removed one by accident? An **Undo** button shows at the top of the queue (Up next, and Now playing's queue) and puts it back as it was, saying what it undoes (\"Undo: shuffled\"). The last ten changes are kept, so you can step back through them. The song that's playing is never interrupted. Starting something new (a song tapped, another queue) isn't a change to undo.",
    "- **Changes waiting to sync:** a synced playlist you have changed since the last sync says **⇄ SYNCED · CHANGES WAITING** in every list of playlists, so you can see what the other device hasn't had yet. It goes back to ⇄ SYNCED after the next Sync (or by itself with Auto playlist sync). It is only said while a phone and PC are paired.",
    "- **Tidied inside:** 28 pieces of code nothing used any more were taken out. Nothing you see changes.",
    "",
    "## 4.0.13 · November 2026",
    "- **Moods into files that kept refusing them (phone):** writing moods could end with \"48 couldn't be written: its tag is of a kind the app leaves alone\" and no more. Three kinds of file were being turned away without a try: WAV files, MP3s whose tag the app's own writer can't read, and songs downloaded as Opus or M4A (from YouTube Music). They are now written through the tag library, in their own format, as your other files are. And when a file still can't take a mood, the message says what kind of file it is and what stood in the way (All files access not allowed, the file not where the phone says, the phone not letting the app change it), with the files' real names in Settings › Problems instead of numbers.",
    "",
    "## 4.0.12 · November 2026",
    "- **Playlists that sync (Settings › Remote & sync):** one list of all your playlists, each with a switch. The ones switched on are kept the same on your phone and PC; the rest stay on the device they are on. Nothing is sent when you flip a switch: Sync now, in the same section, sends them. **Listen later** and the **discovery playlists** can be switched on there too. A synced playlist's songs come together: what you add or take out on the phone is on the PC's, and the other way. Playlists each device made by itself are joined too: two synced playlists of the same name become one with the songs of both, each song once; so do the two Listen laters, and the two discovery playlists for an artist (a song heard to the end on either device is off the list to hear). Nothing is lost by joining. Smart playlists can sync too: the rules go across, and each device fills the playlist from its own music. **Switch them all on** sets every playlist you have to sync in one tap. Queues don't sync. Both devices need this version.",
    "- **Send a playlist to your other device (Settings › Remote & sync, or a playlist's menu › Send a copy):** copies one playlist to the phone or the PC, once. It isn't a sync: the two go their own ways afterwards. The other device adds the songs it can play (the ones that stream, and its own copy of each of your files); a song that is only a file on the sending device is left out and named, and Settings keeps the list of what was left out. Listen later lands on the other's Listen later, a discovery playlist in its discovery playlist for that artist, and a playlist whose name is there already gets only the songs it hasn't. From the phone it goes at once; from the PC the phone takes it within 20 seconds when its app is open on the same network.",
    "- **Auto playlist sync (Settings › Remote & sync):** a switch, off until you turn it on. With it on, your synced playlists sync by themselves: the moment the phone's app finds the PC's (both open, on the same network), and again within about 20 seconds whenever a song is added or taken out on either while both are open. Only the playlists go across this way; plays, favorites, settings and the rest still wait for Sync now. Turning it on on either device is enough. It can't sync while one app is closed or the two are on different networks: they catch up the next time they meet.",
    "- **Sync says plainly that it waits for you:** a synced playlist is the same on both devices after a Sync, not as you go. Without Auto playlist sync, songs you add or take out cross only when you tap Sync now. Settings says so in full view under Playlists that sync, and so does the message when you switch a playlist on.",
    "- **New playlists are set to sync:** any playlist you make from now on (normal, smart, a discovery playlist, Listen later) is set to sync, since that is what you nearly always want. **Settings › Your files › New playlists sync** turns that off; one playlist can be kept to its device with its switch, or made with **New playlist for this device only**. The playlists you had before aren't changed: use Switch them all on.",
    "- **You can see which playlists sync:** every list of playlists (Playlists, Smart playlists, Discovery, Listen later, Add to playlist) marks each one **⇄ SYNCED**, or **THIS DEVICE ONLY** in grey.",
    "",
    "## 4.0.11 · November 2026",
    "- **Other device's songs (Settings › Remote & sync):** keeps your phone's and your PC's music the same without moving files across or downloading everything twice. At each Sync the two tell each other every song they have (names only: no files cross). Each then lists the songs the other has that it hasn't, as a checklist: tick the ones you want, and Options › Download gets them from your streaming service into My music; Options › Leave out takes the rest off the list for good (Bring back undoes that). Songs are matched as Most played matches them, however each device's files are tagged. Nothing is downloaded by itself. Both devices need this version.",
    "- **Every menu entry can go on your bar:** making a layout of your own, the bar's buttons offered only some of the app's screens (Discovery, for one, was nowhere to pick). Everything in the ☰ menu can be put on the bar now: Discovery, Listen later, Downloads, EPs, Singles, Folders, Smart playlists, Mood lists, New releases, Pinned, Skips, the FLAC tabs, Lyrics and the rest.",
    "- **Why a mood couldn't be written:** writing moods into the files could end with \"48 couldn't be written\" and nothing more. It now says why (the kind of file, or what the phone answered), and Settings › Problems lists the reasons with how many files each, and a few of the files by name.",
    "",
    "## 4.0.10 · November 2026",
    "- **A playlist sorts by what its songs are now:** a playlist keeps each song as it was when you put it in. A song you later moved from a single into its album or EP (or gave a tidier artist name) was still sorted under the old one, so sorted by album it sat apart from the album it belongs to. Sorting now goes by each song's tags as they are today.",
    "- **A smart playlist of all your music:** the rule \"Downloaded or my files\" could only say \"is downloaded\" or \"is one of my files\", so a playlist meant to hold your whole library, made from \"is one of my files\", left out every song downloaded in the app. It has a third choice now, **is either (all my music)**: every song you have, however it got there. A live playlist with it grows as you download.",
    "- **Refresh your music folders (Settings › Your music):** a Refresh button reads your folders again when you ask. They are also read again by themselves when downloads finish: after one download, or once the last of a big batch is in.",
    "- **The song count in Settings adds up:** Folders said \"3,280 songs found\" while the folder chooser showed 3,322 for the same folder. Both were right: your downloads are files in that folder too, but the app keeps them as downloads, apart from the files it finds. Settings now says every song in My music and how it divides (files found, and downloads).",
    "- **Coming back to an artist's page is lighter still:** your songs and albums of theirs are kept with the rest of the page while your music hasn't changed, instead of being gathered from all your songs on every return.",
    "",
    "## 4.0.9 · November 2026",
    "- **Back is smooth:** coming back from an album or a song to an artist's page or a search no longer stutters. A list that is in memory now opens straight at the place you were (it used to open at the top, then jump), and an artist's page keeps what it found for half an hour, so it shows at once on coming back and nothing is looked up again.",
    "- **Write moods into the files is always on the Moods tab:** the button used to show only when it had something to write, so it was often simply not there. It is always there now, with Prevent auto mood scans on or off. It writes the moods the app has worked out into the files that have none, and never touches a file that has moods already: those change only when you clear them with the buttons for that and scan again. With nothing to write, tapping it says why (not scanned yet, or files the app isn't allowed to change). Beside it, **Overwrite the moods that differ** counts the files that hold other moods than the app has worked out since, and replaces those when you ask (it asks first). The tab also says separately how many files are empty and how many hold other moods than the app worked out.",
    "- **The Moods tab opens without holding the app still:** all its counting (every mood's songs, what is scanned, what is left to write) was done on the screen's own thread as the tab opened. It is done beside it now, and kept, so the tab opens at once after the first time and is counted again only when your music or its moods change.",
    "- **A censored title is the same song:** your copy named \"Just Don't Give a F**k\" and the service's \"Just Don't Give A Fuck\" are one song, but as names they differ, so your copy was taken for a song you didn't have: it showed among the streaming songs and offered to download again. When songs are compared, a censored word is now read as the word it stands for (stars, other signs or a gap standing in for the letters left out). Titles are always shown as they are written, and ordinary names with signs in them (P!nk, N.P.D) are left alone.",
    "- **An artist's page folds, like Settings:** its sections (Albums and singles, Featured on, Every song, Your songs, Top songs, Left out of this page) are rows that fold open and shut, each saying how many it holds, and all are shut when the page opens: you see what there is and open the one you want, with no scrolling past the others. What's left out is a section like the rest, not a button at the very end. Which ones you opened is kept while you go into an album and come back.",
    "- **Listen later:** a new tab by Discovery (☰ menu), for music you want to hear but can't right now. Pick songs, albums or EPs anywhere, open Options and choose **Add to Listen later playlist**; they wait on the tab, the last added first, with Play, Shuffle and the usual Sort. Options on the tab has **Remove from Listen later**. Unlike a discovery playlist it holds only what you put on it. It is kept as a playlist of yours named Listen later, so it is with your other playlists too.",
    "- **Hide on an artist's page works, and at once:** picking releases far down an artist's page and tapping Hide did nothing: the bar's options were made by a row at the top of the list, which isn't there once you've scrolled past it, so they were for nothing ticked. They now come from the page itself and act on what is ticked when you tap. What you hide disappears as you hide it, with no need to leave the page and come back, and a message says how many were hidden.",
    "- **Album cover's colour, for the visualizer:** a new colour in every visualizer colour menu (the Visualizer's, Now playing's, a style of your own). It follows the cover of what's playing, whatever look you have on: any look with a visualizer in the cover's colour. A cover too dark to see is lifted, its hue kept. The other way round was there already: the Now playing look puts the whole app in the cover's colours, and the visualizer can then take any colour you pick.",
    "- **Leave things out of Personalization Preset Shuffle:** under the shuffle's switch, each kind of choice a preset holds (the look, the theme, the layout, the effects, the Now playing visual, the visualizer, its colour, the Wrapped style, the lyrics card) can be left out. What you leave out stays as you set it while the shuffle changes the rest: shuffle the visualizers and keep your look, say. Picking a preset yourself still puts on all of it.",
    "",
    "## 4.0.8 · November 2026",
    "- **A song that goes silent partway starts again by itself (phone):** one 24-bit song played with sound for about twenty seconds, then went on counting in silence until it was moved. Moving it brought the sound back, so the app now does that itself: when music stops coming out though the song is playing, it starts the song again from the same place within a few seconds, and Settings › Problems says where the sound stopped.",
    "- **Featured on, on an artist's page:** between their albums and singles and Every song, a list of other artists' songs that feature them, so the page has everything they made and not only their own discography. It is a list of songs: one feature never brings in the other artist's whole album; a release is shown only when every song on it features them. A song they lead, with or without guests, stays in their own list. Found from the releases they appear on, the service's search for their name, their top songs and (with a Genius artist picked) Genius's list. A feature can be hidden like any other song.",
    "- **Your songs on an artist's page, as songs or as releases:** a dropdown by its Sort menu shows your songs of theirs as a list of songs, as before, or as your albums, EPs and singles of theirs, so with a big discography you tap the album instead of scrolling to it. Your choice is kept, and the one Sort menu applies to both: the releases follow the same sort as the songs (most played is the release whose songs you played most).",
    "- **Plays on the rows when sorted by plays:** on an artist's page, with Your songs or Every song sorted by Most or Least played, each song's row says how often you played it (your releases too, added up).",
    "- **Back on Now playing goes back to where you were:** from a search, an artist's page, a playlist or any other list, going to Now playing and pressing Back now returns you to that list as you left it (the page, your place's own search). Before, Back opened the menu and the list was gone. When you weren't anywhere before (the app opened on Now playing), Back opens the menu as it did.",
    "- **Back to where you were, on every list:** opening something from far down a list (an album from an artist's page, an artist from a playlist, a search result) and pressing Back returned you to the top of that list. Every screen's list now keeps its place: Back returns you to where you had scrolled to. Coming back to a tab later finds it where you left it too; a page opened afresh starts at its top.",
    "- **Local or streaming, on an artist's releases:** with Everything showing, each album, EP and single says under its year whether it is yours (LOCAL: your files or downloads) or plays from a service (STREAMING), marked the way a song's FLAC is. A release you have only part of says BOTH, with how many of its songs are local and how many are streaming.",
    "- **Your songs on an artist's page are only yours:** the list also held songs of theirs you don't have (ones the app knew from your listening history or a service's library). It now lists only what is on the device: your files and downloads. The song count at the top of the page counts the same.",
    "- **The Tagger skips streaming songs, and says so:** tagging a selection that has streaming songs in it (an album you have part of, picked on an artist's page) tags only the songs you have, and a message says how many streaming songs were skipped and that they have to be downloaded to be tagged.",
    "- **Pick several releases on an artist's page:** hold an album, EP or single (or tap Select by the Sort menu) and tick as many as you like. The bar at the bottom pins them all, hides them all from the page (or puts hidden ones back), and its Options has everything a song's Options has, for all their songs. Songs on the page are picked as anywhere else, and their Options has Hide from this artist's page.",
    "",
    "## 4.0.7 · November 2026",
    "- **An artist's whole discography:** a release could be missing from an artist's page (an older EP, say). YouTube Music's artist page shows only the first few of each kind, and the app never asked for the rest: it does now. And the releases Genius lists for the artist that the service still leaves out are added, marked \"on Genius\"; open one and its songs play from your copy, or are looked for on Tidal and YouTube.",
    "- **Singles that are on an album or EP already are folded away (streaming only):** on an artist's page a service's single whose song is on one of their albums or EPs is left out, so the song is found on its release and the list is shorter. In a service's search, a song found both as its single and on a release shows as the release's, and the single is not listed among the albums. An EP always stays, also when an album has its songs. Your own files are never touched by this: a single you have stays in your music. What was left out is under Show what's left out, and can be put back.",
    "- **Every song of an artist shows by itself:** the Show every song button is gone. Every song is listed right under their albums and singles as soon as the page has gone through their releases, above your songs and their top songs. With Only my music picked, it shows only what you have, as before.",
    "- **Only streaming, on an artist's page:** a third choice in the menu at the top, beside Everything and Only my music: what the services have, without your own files.",
    "- **Sort an artist's page as the other tabs:** a Sort menu for their releases (newest, oldest, by name), for your songs (title, album, added, most and least played, last played, length, shuffled) and for Every song. Each is kept.",
    "- **Genius search finds artists and releases too:** it used to list only songs. It now shows artists, then albums, EPs and singles, and a song by itself only when it was released by itself: a song on an album or EP shows as that release, which opens with all its songs. Tapping one of several same-named artists opens that name's page sorted with the one you tapped.",
    "- **A song that went silent partway (phone):** the sound could stop a little way into a song while the song went on counting. If the equalizer's numbers ever run away (a band at or over half the song's sample rate could make them), they are set back at once and the music goes on, and a band can no longer be built that high. The same for the player's own volume: a crossfade or the DJ turns it down and brings it back, and if one was ever cut short nothing brought it back; a volume left down with nothing fading is now put back within a few seconds (on the computer too). If it happens again, Settings › Problems now says exactly what turned the sound down.",
    "- **Write moods into the files is back on the Moods tab:** with Prevent auto mood scans on, the tab said songs were waiting to have their moods written but had no button for it. The button is there now, also with that switch on (the switch stops what the app does by itself, not what you ask for), and beside Scan moods when songs are still to be scanned. It only fills in files that have no moods in them yet (a file that has moods keeps them), from songs already scanned, and scans nothing.",
    "- **Picking a song in the queue picks that one only:** with the same song waiting in the queue more than once, holding one of them picked every one. Each row of the queue is now its own: pick one and only it is ticked, and Remove from the queue takes out just the rows you picked.",
    "",
    "## 4.0.6 · November 2026",
    "- **Hold a song and picking starts:** holding a song (right-click on a computer) starts multi-select at once with that song picked, everywhere: lists, grids and the queue. Before, some places opened the song's menu and Select was at the bottom of it. A song's own menu is its ⋮; while picking, holding a song opens the options for what's picked.",
    "- **Shuffle shuffles the whole list:** started halfway down a playlist, Shuffle used to mix only what was still to come, so the songs above never played. It now takes every song of the list, with the one playing first and the ones you queued next.",
    "- **Move, don't duplicate (Settings › Playback):** a switch for what Play next and Add to queue do with a song already waiting in the queue. Off, as it always was: it's put in again. On: it's moved to where you put it, so it's in the queue once.",
    "- **The app's messages get out of the way:** tap one, or swipe it to either side, and it's gone, so it never keeps you from Play, Pause or Skip.",
    "- **An artist's page starts with their albums and singles,** then your songs, then their top songs; and your songs are sorted by album until you pick another sort (which is then kept).",
    "- **Every song of an artist:** at the bottom of an artist's page, Show every song goes through all their releases (albums, EPs, singles, and the compilations they are on) and lists each song once. Their top songs are only the service's twenty most played, so a song that is not one of those was nowhere on the page.",
    "- **Two bands with the same name:** when a service has more than one artist under a name, the page offers which one, each with a song it is known for. Your choice is kept, so the name means that band from then on, and the page shows only its songs and albums from the services. Your own files carry only the name, so those still show together.",
    "- **A mixed-up artist sorted with Genius:** some services put two bands of one name under a single artist, songs and albums all mixed (it happens to small, little-known bands). When Genius has more than one artist of that name, the page offers **Sort with Genius**: pick the one you mean, and only the songs and releases on that artist's list on Genius stay. It is a separate switch from the service's own \"which one?\": use either, or both, or turn it off. Show every song then also lists the songs only Genius knows, each once (Genius's separate pages for the album's, the single's and the EP's version of a song count as one).",
    "- **Hide songs and albums from an artist's page yourself:** for when both the service and Genius get it wrong. A song's Options (or an album's menu) has \"Hide from this artist's page\"; pick several songs first to hide them together. Nothing is deleted: **Show what's left out** at the end of the page lists everything hidden or left out by Genius, and \"Show on this artist's page\" puts one back, whatever Genius says. Your own files are never left out unless you hide them.",
    "- **Search Genius:** a new place in the search bar's menu. Genius lists nearly every song, so one that no service has can still be found by name. It has no music itself: a song you have plays from your copy, any other is looked for on Tidal and YouTube when you play it.",
    "- **Streamed songs have their covers in Android Auto:** the cover of a song that streams was handed to the car in a way it could not open, so only downloaded songs and your own files showed one. It is also fetched before the song starts now, so it is there the first time.",
    "- **The music no longer stops when the sound moves between your earbuds and the car (phone):** with both connected, Android tells the app the same thing as when headphones are unplugged, and the song paused partway. It still pauses for a moment, then goes on by itself when something is still connected to play through. Unplugging your only headphones pauses as before.",
    "",
    "## 4.0.5 · November 2026",
    "- **Checking for an update always finishes:** Settings could stay on \"checking for a newer version…\" for good. The check waited its turn among the app's other background work (all of it busy during a scan or a sync) and had no time limit of its own. It now runs by itself, ends within about a minute whatever the network does, and has a second way to find the newest version when GitHub won't give its list. Downloading the update no longer waits for other work either.",
    "",
    "## 4.0.4 · November 2026",
    "- **A song you have is never downloaded twice:** if you already have a song with the same title and artist (whatever its album is tagged as), Download leaves it and says so: your copy is the one that plays. Before, a download whose tags you changed (a single moved into its album) could be taken for a song you didn't have, streamed instead of played from your file, and downloaded again as a second copy. \"Keep both\" (Settings, or when asked about a near match) still downloads it.",
    "- **A download follows its file:** when a downloaded song's file has moved (after a tag change put it in another folder), the app finds your file of that song again and goes on playing it, instead of streaming it.",
    "",
    "## 4.0.3 · November 2026",
    "- **The visualizer works for streamed songs (computer):** it stood still for any song that was streaming rather than playing from a file on that computer (a hi-res stream, or a song you only have on the phone). It listens to the stream now.",
    "- **Pick an artist's or an album's songs whole, in Search:** hold an artist or an album in the search results (right-click on a computer) and all its songs are picked, as songs are; while picking, a tap does the same and a tick shows it. Then play, queue, download, tag or delete them together. In your music it's every song you have of them; on a service, an album's songs and the songs an artist's page lists.",
    "- **The right bit depth under Now playing (computer):** a 24-bit FLAC was shown as \"32-bit\" (the player decodes it into 32-bit numbers, and that was what the app showed). It shows the file's own depth now, and none for MP3 and AAC, which have no bit depth. The 32-bit FLACs tab was right all along.",
    "- **A USB DAC is recognised as the output (Windows):** the app worked out Windows' default output from when a device was last picked by hand, so a DAC Windows chose as it was plugged in (a FiiO KA17) wasn't seen: the Equalizer showed another device and used its settings. The app now asks Windows itself. The music always went to the right device; only the app's idea of it was wrong.",
    "",
    "## 4.0.2 · November 2026",
    "- **A Q button for your queues:** at the top left of Now playing, where \"From the album…\" stood. Beside the other buttons there was only room for \"F…\". Q opens your queues just as those words did.",
    "",
    "## 4.0.1 · November 2026",
    "- **Moods match (Settings › Remote & sync):** a new switch under Tags match, for moods only, with its own main device. At each sync the other device's songs get the main one's moods, written into their files, with nothing scanned again. For when one device's moods were lost or cleared, or when you scan on one and tag on the other.",
    "- **Tags match leaves moods alone:** it used to make its main device lead the moods too. Now the tags and the moods are apart: each follows the device you pick for it.",
    "- **Moods only sync when you ask:** a sync used to carry moods both ways by itself. Now they go across only with Moods match on. Turn it on if you want your two devices' moods kept the same.",
    "- **Tidal's sign-in page opens by itself (computer),** as its text said it did.",
    "- **The music stops after four songs in a row that can't be played** (no connection, a service turning the app away), and says why, instead of skipping on through the whole queue.",
    "- **Linux:** links open in your browser (sign-ins, the update's page, folders); a link that can't be opened is copied instead. With no tray, closing the window minimizes it instead of hiding it for good. Software drawing (Settings › General) for a window that flickers.",
    "",
    "## 4.0.0 · November 2026",
    "- **Rebuilt from the inside:** the app was taken apart and put back together as separate parts (playback, the DJ, your library, downloads, the stats, the sound), each with its own tests. It does what it did, more smoothly and more reliably, and your settings, stats, playlists and presets carry over.",
    "- **Linux:** Radon Player now runs on Linux as well as Windows and Android, as a .deb package that brings mpv and ffmpeg with it. It has the tray, Start with Linux, updates from inside the app, and the keyboard's and a headset's media keys with the desktop's own \"now playing\" controls.",
    "- **Music no longer stops at the end of a song (phone):** with the DJ on, a song heard to its end could leave a long silence before the next. The next song now comes at once.",
    "- **Bit-perfect keeps every bit:** a 24-bit song always reaches a USB DAC as 24-bit (some were brought down to 16-bit first).",
    "- **The DJ's voice is the same everywhere:** one natural voice on the phone and the computer, fetched once from DJ › Voice settings. She no longer says things a voice can't say well (dots for pauses, signs), and places in a row are said in words.",
    "- **Mood sets of their own:** Happy, Chill, Calm, Sad and Dark each have their own DJ set; only Energetic, Party and Hype share one.",
    "- **Moods read better:** the sound is judged on a scale that fits real records, the words count for more, and a song gets four moods at most. Reset moods (Settings) and Scan moods put the new ones in your files.",
    "- **The DJ's facts take turns:** what it tells you goes round by subject, and it knows more about your collection.",
    "- **Smart shuffle knows more:** \"haven't heard in a while\" now goes by your plays everywhere (Last.fm and your imports), not only the plays in the app.",
